# GOV-EOL-PIN-1 generalised (r187): the governance checker parses Acceptance
# Criteria with '(?m)^...**AC[^\r\n]*$', and .NET's multiline $ never matches
# before a CRLF. With core.autocrlf=true a fresh checkout rewrote every task
# file to CRLF, which made every AC line invisible - 60 files were in that state
# and a clean clone would have made it all 320. The AC rules only work on LF, so
# the line ending is part of the contract, not a preference.
#
# Same reason the existing eol=lf pins above exist: recorded sha256 evidence
# breaks when autocrlf rewrites a file, and the fix is to stop the rewriting.
doc/tasks/*.md text eol=lf

# GOV-UNTRACKED-BACKLOG-DISPOSITION-1: the disposition evidence is sha256-recorded, so
# pin LF on checkout exactly like the GOV-UNTRACKED-BACKLOG-1 pins above. The relocated
# anomaly payloads are the opposite case: their SHA-256 *is* the original content
# fingerprint, so they are marked -text to guarantee Git never rewrites their bytes.
verification/GOV-UNTRACKED-BACKLOG-DISPOSITION-1/*.json text eol=lf
verification/GOV-UNTRACKED-BACKLOG-DISPOSITION-1/*.md text eol=lf
verification/GOV-UNTRACKED-BACKLOG-DISPOSITION-1/anomalies/** -text
